51
00:34:30.000 --> 00:35:20.000
five-star resort and just stayed there until my savings account was dry. I had a terrific time, but I had to come back and face the consequences, which was not so great. So it took my doctors seeing that and seeing, oh, yeah, I guess she gets manic. So maybe she's bipolar. That's what made the difference. So, Dr. Mikkelowitz, I want to ask you, what are the areas of research that you're most excited about right now in terms of improving treatment for people with bipolar disorder? What are the big questions that you would like to see answered? First, we're getting more and more interested in early intervention and prevention. If we can catch kids early enough who are just showing the early warning signs, particularly those who have a family history of the

52
